-- title:
--   Conjugators agreeing up to powers of r differ by a scalar
-- statement:
--   Let $r$ and $\bar r$ be primes with $\bar r \neq r$, let $N$ be a natural number, and let $a_1, b_1 \in \mathbb{Q}$ be such that the quaternion algebra $B_1 = \mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a_1, b_1]$ satisfies `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt` $\bar r$, i.e. $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$ and, for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, every nonzero element of $B_1 \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ is a unit exactly when $\bar r \in v$. Let $\Lambda_1, R_1$ be $\mathbb{Z}$-submodules of $B_1$ with $R_1 \le \Lambda_1$, where $\Lambda_1$ is an order maximal among orders containing it, and where $R_1$ is an intersection of two such maximal orders with relative index $N$ in the first of them. Let $v$ be a height-one prime of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ containing $r$, let $K_0$ be a field of characteristic zero, and let $\iota_0 : B_1 \to M_2(K_0)$ be an injective $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra map. Let $\Gamma^{t}$ be a subgroup of $B_1^{\times}$ whose elements are precisely those of [`CerednikDrinfeld.CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_CosetGraphAtPrime.html#L37), the intersection over all height-one primes $w \neq v$ of the preimages, under the map $B_1^{\times} \to (B_1 \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_w)^{\times}$, of the subgroups generated by the units of the local box of $R_1$ at $w$. Finally let $g, g' \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ be such that for every $\gamma \in \Gamma^{t}$ there are integers $k, k'$ with $r^{k}\, g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1} = r^{k'}\, g'\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g'^{-1}$ in $M_2(K_0)$. Then there is $c \in K_0^{\times}$ with $g' = c \cdot g$, the scalar being the image of $c$ under the units map of $\mathrm{algebraMap}\ K_0\ M_2(K_0)$, and moreover $g' u g'^{-1} = g u g^{-1}$ for every $u \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$.
--
--   A rigidity statement for the two-dimensional representation of the away-unit group attached to an Eichler order in a definite quaternion algebra: a conjugator of $\iota_0$ on $\Gamma^{t}$ is unique up to a central scalar, even allowing ambiguity by powers of $r$. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the construction, where conjugation by such a $g$ rigidifies a fake elliptic curve and the resulting conjugation action must be shown independent of the choice of $g$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_QuaternionAlgebra_exists_eq_units_map_mul_and_forall_conj_eq_of_forall_exists_zpow_smul_conj_eq_of_awayUnits.lean

